111
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:51,080
The first question I have for you is, is there ever a reason to unpack a wound in the field?

112
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:55,360
And the reason I asked that question is that a lot of folks on the EMS side are taught

113
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:58,360
never to take it dressing down once it's packed.

114
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:01,080
And I'm just curious to know your thoughts on that.

115
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:06,520
I think whenever you look at what we do in the OR, you know, say someone has a gunshot

116
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:11,280
wound to the abdomen and, you know, we take them up there and we, you know, open them

117
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:12,280
up.

118
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:14,800
First thing we do is try to evacuate all the clots, right?

119
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:19,680
Kind of get all the clots out and then we pack the abdomen with a bunch of laparotomy

120
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:20,680
pads.

121
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:25,400
But then we take them out and we're going to try to identify any sort of bleeding.

122
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:26,880
So I think it's appropriate.

123
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:32,360
I think if you look at the, in fact, if you look at the guidelines, at least in the T-C-C

124
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:37,640
guidelines, it says that you can pack or un, you know, leave packed and then repack around

125
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:39,960
it or just, you know, pack new.

126
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:42,660
I'm more in favor of repacking.

127
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:44,240
So I would pull stuff out.

128
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:48,760
I pulled the clots out and tried to identify where the bleeding is because clearly if it's

129
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:53,160
still bleeding through either I'm not effective or I need to do some other intervention.

130
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:55,920
So I don't really know until I actually get a look at it.

131
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:56,920
Right.

132
00:07:56,920 --> 00:07:57,920
That's fair.

133
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:01,760
I think a lot of times on the EMS side when we see blood coming through a dressing, we

134
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:03,360
just layer it up.

135
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:05,000
But what we need is directed pressure.

136
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,600
We can't do that if there's a bunch of stuff.

137
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:08,600
Yeah.

138
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:10,720
I think direct pressure works overall.

139
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:15,040
I think you can do it on top of dressings, but I think if you continuously kind of layer

140
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:19,000
it on, later on, later on, it becomes, it does become less effective because now you're

141
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,280
kind of trying to distribute this pointed focus of pressure onto a more, a broader surface,

142
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:27,200
which it becomes effective.

143
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:35,360
So either can apply direct pressure on the wound or you can pack effectively and hopefully

144
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:37,640
not have to repack.

145
00:08:37,640 --> 00:08:42,560
But some wounds do actually, will bleed through and what are you going to do?

146
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:43,560
Put a tourniquet.

147
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:44,560
Right.

148
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:45,560
Right.

149
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:47,880
And that leads me to my next question along these same lines.

150
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:50,040
What about taking a tourniquet down in the field?

151
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:52,840
Yeah, I'm confused why EMS doesn't do that now.

152
00:08:52,840 --> 00:09:00,560
I think if you look at overall, there's a lot of tourniquets that are just, they're

153
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:07,360
applied rapidly in a certain situation because of the level of training based on the responder,

154
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:11,880
whether it be law enforcement bystander, maybe even EMS or fire.

155
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:16,800
But there are a lot of wounds that are clearly, it's not indicated.

156
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:22,240
And I think it would be beneficial for EMS to learn how to pick those down and just evaluate

157
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:26,600
them, even if it's just like, Hey, I'm going to go ahead and pack this wound and then I'm

158
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:28,640
going to take the drink it down and see how it looks.

159
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:29,640
Right.

160
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:33,640
And this is something that has been a topic of conversation for a long time on the wilderness

161
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:38,320
medicine side of things, but I haven't seen it cross over much to the civilian side.

162
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:45,360
I think we'll hopefully see some changes soon.

163
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:48,640
You know, some people from here, from the University of Mexico, we recently submitted

164
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:53,720
a paper that kind of discusses the idea of converting tourniquets in the pre-hospital

165
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:59,320
setting because, you know, especially here in places like this where evacs maybe, you

166
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:01,640
know, four or five hours.

167
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:06,800
So why shouldn't we, from my perspective, you know, why shouldn't we be teaching, you

168
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:11,320
know, EMS professionals to take down a wound or take down a tourniquet and evaluate the

169
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:16,720
wound if it needs a tourniquet grade, if it needs a pressure dressing grade, or it just,

170
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:21,360
you know, kind of a reactionary sort of situation, then that's fine too.

171
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:22,520
But we're getting it down early.

172
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:25,080
And what about a time limit on that?

173
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:30,240
If we can get someone to a trauma center, say a level one in an hour, two hours, don't

174
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:31,400
worry about it.

175
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:35,400
Or is it something where like time is always relevant and you should take a tourniquet

176
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:36,400
down if you can.

177
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:39,080
No, my personal belief is that it's always relevant.

178
00:10:39,080 --> 00:10:43,880
And you know, clearly the data suggests that up to two hours is perfectly safe.

179
00:10:43,880 --> 00:10:50,440
But at the same time, you know, I'd like to fully use the full abilities of our EMS

180
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:51,440
professionals.

181
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:55,000
And if that is, you know, recognizing the fact that a tourniquet is going to be up,

182
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,960
then we should take it down.

183
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:01,240
One it's, it's not a benign intervention, even though it's, you know, quote unquote,

184
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:04,000
safe for two hours, it's not benign.

185
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,640
And additionally, it's painful.

186
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:10,200
And so, you know, do if I don't need it on, what's the reason?

187
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:14,160
And then if you look at the overall level response that you get from a tourniquet application,

188
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:22,060
no matter where you are, it's going to be a significant trauma level response that may

189
00:11:22,060 --> 00:11:31,440
not necessarily be beneficial to the patient in terms of logistics and cost and overall

190
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:32,440
required intervention.

191
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:35,040
Yeah, that definitely makes sense.

192
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:38,520
So for folks in the field, if you get to the point where you've had a chance to get your

193
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:43,080
head up and think about reevaluating your interventions, like if you have time for a

194
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:45,800
secondary assessment, think about taking it down.

195
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:46,800
Yeah, exactly.

196
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:49,440
It's very similar to like tactical field care, right?

197
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:53,840
So you know, I'm going to kind of step or take a step back.

198
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:56,960
I'm going to kind of look at what we've done, say look at the injuries, kind of go from

199
00:11:56,960 --> 00:12:00,320
head to toe again.

200
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:04,200
You know, someone shows up in the ED, they, you know, we go through a whole set of different

201
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:08,800
stuff before the tourniquet ever is a question of coming down.

202
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:13,800
So yeah, take the opportunity to investigate everything, determine if the patient is stable,

203
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:19,880
and if so, I think it should be a reasonable option to expect paramedics to intervene.

204
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:21,120
Cool.

205
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:22,600
Next question about bleeding.

206
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:26,440
What are your thoughts on carrying hemostats to clamp arterial bleeding?

207
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:27,800
That's a terrible idea.

208
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:30,520
I don't, I came in and entertained that question.

209
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:31,520
It's absolutely awesome.

210
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:33,640
How do you look cool if you don't carry hemostats in your flight suit?

211
00:12:33,720 --> 00:12:37,760
Yeah, I mean, but if you look at the, even look at the ATLS guidelines, right?

212
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:39,800
So ATLS says do not clank.

213
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:45,520
You will, there's the potential for injuring other underlying structures and overall completely

214
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:48,920
missing the vessel is significant.

215
00:12:48,920 --> 00:12:54,000
So and I think a lot of the time the perception is that like having hemostats to clamp on

216
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,640
artery will be straightforward and that when you clamp it, there won't be collateral damage.

217
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:03,880
My understanding is that it's quite hard to identify like where you need to clamp and

218
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:07,800
then those jagged edges on the hemostats can cause injury as well.

219
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:08,800
Yeah.

220
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:15,120
Hey, you know, we take people to the operating room and we often have to make large incisions

221
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:19,000
in order to access approximately the control of the vessel.

222
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,680
And then we look at, you know, at that point, controlling the hemorrhage and decide how

223
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:26,820
we're going to repair it if we need to repair it.

224
00:13:26,820 --> 00:13:30,060
So the idea that like, I'm just going to go ahead and just going to stick the hemostat

225
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:35,100
in here and clamp it is foolish and a bit naive.

226
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:40,140
You know, I'm going to be so bold to say that I didn't carry hemostats at one point when

227
00:13:40,140 --> 00:13:46,500
I was, you know, in the pre-hospital setting that I didn't think it was a viable option.

228
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:48,500
So you know, that's my point.

229
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:50,980
Like I've been there, I thought it was a good idea.

230
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:56,380
And I say, that's a good idea, but it's something that I could do, you know, and I think I've

231
00:13:56,380 --> 00:14:03,140
got to the point now, based upon the evidence is like, nah, I can either pack, I can hold

232
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:06,340
pressure or I can put on a lip tourniquet.

233
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:07,340
Right.

234
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:08,340
Yeah.

235
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:10,780
